  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1248 AM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nSunday night through Friday:\n\nHigh pressure will move east of the region Sunday night into \nMonday, with increasing southeast winds on the backside of the \nhigh keeping dry air in the lower portions of the air column. \nThis may help shunt back any advancing showers and storms to \nlater Monday or more likely Monday evening. Thus, chances for \nprecipitation are now lower (below 30 percent) in the eastern \nparts of the area, and may need to be lowered more in the west \n(currently 40 to 70 percent) in later forecasts, if this dry \nlook in the model trends continues. Chilly lows in the 40s \nSunday night should rebound into the upper 60s to around 70 \ndegrees on Monday. \n\nModels and ensembles suggest that the main area of showers and \nthunderstorms should move into western parts of the area Monday \nevening and elsewhere later Monday night. The low level jet feed\nof moisture and upward vertical motion, along the surface warm \nfront, should help bring rounds of showers and some storms to \nthe area into at least Tuesday morning. Forecast soundings are \nshowing moist adiabatic profiles with tall skinny mean layer \nCAPE that is modest at best and high precipitable water values \napproaching or over 2.00 inches. Given this setup, some training\nof convection may be possible, and locally heavy rainfall may \noccur. \n\nThe deep layer (30 to 40 knots) bulk shear values are fairly \nrobust, though the poor lapse rates and tall skinny CAPE may \nfavor more heavy rainfall than strong to severe storms. Could \nstill see stronger storms with some gusty winds occur in this \nsetup. Will continue to message the heavy rainfall and perhaps \nsome stronger storms later Monday into Monday night/Tuesday. \n\nThere is more uncertainty if another round of showers and \nstorms with some heavy rainfall would occur later Tuesday into \nWednesday. The deterministic models are trending further south \nof the area with this next round of precipitation, as high \npressure to the north shunts this activity southward. Ensembles \nstill have a fairly wet period during this time, though they may\nnot have caught onto this trend yet. For now, will leave higher\nchances (40 to 70 percent) for showers and storms for Tuesday \ninto Wednesday, but adjustments downward may be needed if this \ndrier trend continues.\n\nThe high may linger through a good portion of the rest of next \nweek, so this drier trend may linger as well. Ensembles suggest \ntemperatures may remain near or a little below seasonal normal \nvalues. \n\nWood",
